  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 156 AM CDT Sat Oct 3 2026\n\nFrost and sub-freezing temperatures down into the lower 30s \nwill wrap up around 7 AM this morning across portions of \nNorthwest Wisconsin and the Minnesota Arrowhead. A cloudy day is\nin store today as an upper-level trough and surface low move \nacross the upper Mississippi Valley. Initial rain showers moving\nover the International Border and Iron Range will encounter dry\nlow-level air, with much of the early radar echoes falling as \nvirga before the atmosphere saturates. Scattered rain showers \nwill continue on and off throughout the morning and afternoon. \nWeak elevated instability along with precipitable water values \nhovering around an inch may generate a few more robust \nconvective showers and perhaps an isolated rumble of thunder. \nSevere weather is not anticipated. Rainfall totals will \ngenerally stay under a quarter of an inch, with many places \nseeing less than a tenth of an inch. Southerly winds will pick \nup today, gusting between 20 and 30 mph at times.\n\nPrecipitation will taper off tonight as the cold front moves \nthrough, yielding clearing skies from west to east. Cool and \nblustery post-frontal conditions take over on Sunday. Northwest \nwinds will be gusty, particularly across the Arrowhead region \nwhere gusts of 20 to 30 mph are forecast. While some wraparound \ncloudiness and isolated sprinkles are possible early in the day,\nthe region will stay predominantly dry with high temperatures \nreaching the upper 50s to lower 60s.\n\nHigh pressure will build into the region Sunday night, setting \nup strong radiational cooling. Inland locations will see \ntemperatures drop into the 28 to 32 degree range, bringing \nanother widespread frost and freeze to the Northland. Areas \nimmediately adjacent to Lake Superior and parts of the Brainerd \nLakes area may remain slightly warmer. Southerly winds return on\nMonday with abundant sunshine, and a warming trend will push \nafternoon high temperatures into the upper 60s and lower 70s by \nTuesday and Wednesday. A weak trough passing around mid-week \nwill bring minimal rain chances before slightly cooler air \nsettles in toward the end of the week.",
